py for insomnia and suggests against melatonin or valerian. - VA/DoD chronic insomnia guideline, recommendations 8 and 14, 2025.":["u49"],"copy|When falls are the concern, the case integrates physical therapy, home safety and review of sedating medicines with movement-based options.":["u50"],"copy|Tai chi combines attention, balance and movement practice.":["u51"],"copy|Someone who asks about a supplement for balance may also have a modifiable drug exposure or a home hazard (p339, p343).":["u52"],"copy|In Figure 24-1 the older adult is at the center of a comprehensive geriatric assessment that includes gait, frailty, cognition, sleep, mood, medicines, health behavior and social support.":["u53"],"copy|The surrounding domains of mind, body, community and spirit explain why an intervention can have value beyond the symptom for which it was first considered.":["u54"],"copy|A supervised movement class may provide balance practice and social contact.":["u55"],"copy|Conversely, an additional capsule for “healthy aging” can worsen pill burden without affecting the activity the patient wants to preserve.":["u56"],"copy|Define the intended outcome before deciding which evidence is relevant (p329–331).":["u57"],"copy|Figure 24-2 separates four broad categories.":["u58"],"copy|Dietary supplements include botanicals, vitamins and other ingested products.":["u59"],"copy|Mind-body practices include yoga, tai chi, qigong and meditation.":["u60"],"copy|Manual approaches include chiropractic care, massage, osteopathic techniques and acupuncture as grouped by the authors.":["u61"],"copy|Ayurveda and traditional Chinese medicine are whole systems incorporating several approaches.":["u62"],"copy|A positive trial of a specific movement program does not validate an ingested product from the same tradition.":["u63"],"copy|Equally, an adverse effect of one herbal formulation does not establish the risk of a supervised exercise class.":["u64"],"copy|Identify the actual intervention before assigning benefit or harm (p329–331).":["u65"],"copy|Table 24-1 broadens the history.":["u66"],"copy|Ask what the patient thinks is causing the problem, what stress or support affects it, what has already been tried and which conventional or complementary practitioners are involved.":["u67"],"copy|Include nutrition, activity, sleep, substance use, readiness for change and beliefs that help the patient cope.":["u68"],"copy|In the medication history, name teas, powders, vitamins, herbal preparations and products obtained without a prescription.":["u69"],"copy|Ask to see the package, including concentration and all ingredients, and establish how the patient actually uses it.":["u70"],"copy|A patient can reasonably answer “no” to taking additional medicines while taking several products regarded as food or as part of a daily routine (p331–332).":["u71"],"copy|The chapter describes U.S. dietary supplements as a category distinct from approved medicines for specific diseases.":["u72"],"copy|Manufacturing requirements and adverse-event reporting do not establish clinical benefit before a product is sold.":["u73"],"copy|A brand name can encompass preparations with different constituent amounts or extracts.":["u74"],"copy|Record dose, formulation, indication, frequency and perceived effect alongside other medicines.":["u75"],"copy|This is particularly important for a person taking anticoagulants, psychoactive drugs or multiple products with overlapping ingredients.":["u76"],"copy|An interaction may affect risk even when the supplement itself has little evidence of efficacy for the advertised use (p331–333).":["u77"],"copy|Table 24-2 lists resources for different questions.":["u78"],"copy|The NIH Office of Dietary Supplements provides ingredient information, while NCCIH covers complementary approaches more broadly.":["u79"],"copy|A database of natural products can help with proposed interactions.":["u80"],"copy|Independent testing can help distinguish contents or quality but d