ed results from 39 trials involving 20,827 people with conditions including musculoskeletal pain, headache and osteoarthritis.":["u167"],"copy|Benefits persisted beyond treatment in the studies discussed, but the comparison with sham procedures is a different contrast from adding treatment to usual care.":["u168"],"copy|Ask which pain limits the patient, whether relief would improve participation in a valued activity and whether attending treatment is practical.":["u169"],"copy|Combine assessment of benefit with a review of concurrent therapy and the practitioner delivering the treatment (p341–342).":["u170"],"copy|Ayurveda groups individualized dietary, exercise, lifestyle and other treatments within a medical system originating in the Indian subcontinent.":["u171"],"copy|Research on a single herb does not test the entire approach.":["u172"],"copy|The chapter warns that some preparations contain metals or minerals with potential for toxicity.":["u173"],"copy|Traditional Chinese medicine likewise includes diet, herbs, movement, manual methods and acupuncture.":["u174"],"copy|Studies of a whole treatment package cannot identify which component caused a change.":["u175"],"copy|Ask for the full preparation and the other treatments given; respect the person's beliefs while assessing dose, interactions and plausible benefit separately for each exposure (p342).":["u176"],"copy|In the clinical case, an older woman living after a stroke wants to remain independent in the community.":["u177"],"copy|A Timed Up and Go of 15 seconds, a MoCA of 24/30, mild depressive symptoms and limited social support raise different questions.":["u178"],"copy|Ask whether a proposed intervention addresses a limiting function, whether she can carry it out, and whether solving one problem will worsen another.":["u179"],"copy|Figure 24-3 connects physical therapy and a home-safety assessment to fall risk, tai chi to movement and social participation, and review of sedating medicines to gait and fr